 "name": "Corruption and U.S. diplomacy, 1947–1978",
 "authorLine": "Agent run s8, 2026-10-30",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: corruption shaped U.S. policy in three ways — as a reason to withhold or condition aid (China 1947, Korea 1961), as a domestic political liability Washington tried to manage through its allies (South Vietnam 1966–68), and, in 1975–76, as American firms' own payments abroad, which diplomats had to contain or disclose (Lockheed in Germany, the Netherlands, Japan and Saudi Arabia). The argument rests on 28 documents from 12 volumes, 1947–1978, drawn from about 2,200 documents in the 553-volume series that mention corruption. Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came from the State Department's Central Decimal File, Subject-Numeric files and Central Foreign Policy File (RG 59), the Jidda Embassy files (RG 84, Lot 79F80), Deputy Secretary Christopher's records (Lot 81D113, NAID 1487627), and the Kennedy, Johnson, Ford and Carter libraries; the editors cite, unprinted, the Far Eastern Affairs files of Lot 62D26 (NAID 1965953) and the NSC Policy Papers lots 63D351/66D95 (NAID 2839192). What this request cannot get from FRUS: the Church subcommittee's and SEC's own investigative files (24 FRUS documents in 15 volumes name the subcommittee; no bundled shelf); the making of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (4 documents, 1978–80, all after passage); and archival pointers before December 1910, where the citation data begins.",
